{"qid":"Q20","names":["Kingdom of Norway"],"title":"Kingdom of Norway","endonym":{"native":"Kongeriket Norge","lang":"nb"},"capsule":"The modern Kingdom of Norway was established in its current constitutional form on 17 May 1814, when the Eidsvoll Assembly adopted a liberal constitution following the dissolution of the Danish-Norwegian union; the country then entered a personal union with Sweden under the Swedish king until full independence in 1905. The 19th century saw rapid industrialization and emigration, and the union with Sweden was dissolved peacefully on 7 June 1905 when the Norwegian Storting declared independence and invited Prince Carl of Denmark to take the throne as Haakon VII. Norway endured German occupation (1940–45) before emerging as a founding member of NATO in 1949 and developing into one of the world's wealthiest states through North Sea oil revenues from the 1970s onward.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Norway's modern territorial boundaries are precisely established through international treaties and surveys; the 1814 borders with Sweden were fixed by the Treaty of Kiel and subsequent demarcation, making this one of the most confidently mapped polities in the dataset.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Norway","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Norway"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Norway","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Norway"}],"britannica":"place/Norway","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Norway","rulers":[{"qid":"Q155002","name":"Frederick VI of Denmark","start":1808,"end":1814,"image":"Frederik6coronation.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special: